now this brings us to the remains found  this afternoon at colville bay at lake  mead madison kimbrough spoke with two  women who made that startling discovery  she joins us live in the newsroom with  more on what we know so far madison lake  mead is a normal hangout for these  locals of henderson sisters lindsay and  lynette melvin were paddle boarding this  afternoon when they made the call into  park rangers  it looked like a rock from afar but  upon further inspection it looked  clearly like bone and then we kind of  started to move the sand around the  skull just to kind of get an idea of  what it actually was a day at the lake  turns into a startling find as more  human remains were found in the sand at  lake mead this time at caulville bay  where lindsey melvin was paddle boarding  with her sister lynette saturday  afternoon at first i thought it was a  big horn sheep and we kind of started  digging around a little bit as we  discovered the jaw we realized it was  human remains and that's when we  contacted park rangers to come and  investigate for the longest time i was  in disbelief like i did not think that  we actually just came upon human remains  lindsay who's an rn says she's used to  seeing things like this but for it not  to be in the typical hospital setting is  unnerving it's definitely surreal  but  i think there's a larger part of me that  is kind of happy that this person was  found because i'm sure that they do have  family that wants to know what happened  to them and with the water levels  continuing to drop in lake mead the  likelihood of more bodies surfacing is  probable as metro police have already  received numerous tips since the first  discovery on may first  and the clark county coroner's office is  working to identify the remains  discovered today sources tell the 8 news  now i-team there are no signs of foul  play and at this point metro police are  not involved in the investigation live  in the newsroom madison kimbrough live  local now
 

